<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Melikdoms of Karabakh</h1><p class="st-meta">1603 CE – 1822 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Five Armenian principalities in the highlands of Karabakh whose meliks kept local self-rule under Persian overlords until the rise of the Karabakh Khanate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 17th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> recognised local Armenian lords, called meliks, in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, many descended from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-khachen">Principality of Khachen</a>. Five melikdoms, Gulistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jraberd">Jraberd</a>, Khachen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanda">Varanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dizak">Dizak</a>, became known as the Khamsa, the Five.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each melik ruled his district from fortresses with his own troops, collecting taxes for the Persian governor at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja</a>. The meliks maintained churches and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandzasar-monastery">Gandzasar</a> catholicosate. In the 1720s, as Safavid power collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avan-yuzbashi">Avan Yuzbashi</a> led Armenian resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davit-bek">Davit Bek</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-syunik">Syunik</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> later granted the meliks wide autonomy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1750s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panah-ali-khan">Panah Ali Khan</a>, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melik-shahnazar-ii">Melik Shahnazar of Varanda</a>, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> and subordinated the meliks to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a>. Many meliks emigrated to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the last melikdoms disappeared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire's</a> annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> by 1822.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/melikdoms-of-karabakh.jpg" alt="Melikdoms of Karabakh" width="320" height="361"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_principalities_of_karabakh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1603 CE – 1822 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Avan Yuzbashi, Melik Shahnazar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khamsa Melikdoms, Five Melikdoms, Melikdoms of Khamsa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=melikdoms-of-karabakh" aria-label="Melikdoms of Karabakh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5839 734 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Melikdoms of Karabakh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 1003h0M6281 1235h0M6153 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6159 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6159 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Azerbaijan. <a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=melikdoms-of-karabakh">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
